{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Customer Carbon Footprint Tool has new features and an updated methodology - AWS","context":"AWS has announced updates to the Customer Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT), which includes easier access to carbon emissions data, visibility into emissions by AWS Region, and an updated methodology (v2.0). The new feature allows users to export monthly data through AWS's Billing and Cost Management Data Exports service, providing carbon emissions estimates for all member accounts linked to their management account in AWS Organizations","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/04/customer-carbon-footprint-tool-updated-methodology/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS’ Customer Carbon Footprint Tool now includes Scope 3 emissions data - AWS","context":"AWS has updated its Customer Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) to include Scope 3 emissions data and Scope 1 natural gas and refrigerants. This expansion allows AWS customers to have a more comprehensive view of their cloud carbon footprint, covering all three industry-standard emission scopes as defined by the Greenhouse Gas Protocol. The update provides visibility into the lifecycle carbon impact of AWS usage, including emissions from manufacturing servers, powering AWS facilities, and transporting equipment to data centers. Historical data is available from January 2022, enabling organizations to track their progress and make informed decisions about their cloud strategy to meet sustainability goals. The updated CCFT data is accessible through the dashboard and AWS Billing and Cost Management Data Exports, allowing for easy integration into operational workflows, sustainability planning, and reporting processes. For more information, visit the AWS Billing and Cost Management console or read the updated methodology documentation and release notes","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/aws-customer-carbon-footprint-tool-scope-3-emissions-data/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS reduces publishing time for Carbon Footprint Data to 21 days or Less - AWS","context":"AWS has significantly reduced the publishing time for carbon footprint data from up to three months to 21 days or less. This improvement provides users with more timely insights into their carbon emissions and resource usage. The Carbon Footprint Tool (CCFT) dashboard now offers carbon footprint data with estimates published between the 15th and 21st of the month following usage. Additionally, the CCFT dashboard maintains 38 months of data, enabling users to view their carbon usage trends over an extended period. To access the carbon footprint data, users can navigate to their carbon emissions data through the AWS Billing and Cost Management console. For further information about CCFT, users can visit the CCFT capabilities and features page, review the user guides, and explore the CCFT webpage","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/12/aws-reduces-publishing-time-carbon-footprint-data/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"querying-aws-cloudwatch","skill_description":"Runs SQL queries on CloudWatch Logs data exported as Apache Iceberg tables in S3 Tables. Covers VPC Flow Logs, WAF logs, CloudFront access logs, Route 53 resolver logs, Network Firewall logs, EKS audit logs, Verified Access logs, SES logs, VPC Lattice logs, Step Functions logs, NLB access logs, S3 Server Access Logs, and 20+ other AWS vended data sources. Applies when analyzing network traffic, investigating security incidents, querying exported logs with SQL, enabling S3 Tables integration, configuring log export, correlating logs with other data, running Athena queries on the aws-cloudwatch table bucket, analyzing S3 access patterns, S3 latency investigation, S3 cost attribution, or bucket access auditing.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, s3tables, athena, glue, s3\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-cloudwatch"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-marketplace-metering","skill_description":"Deploys, queries, and debugs AWS Marketplace usage-based (PAYG) metering — the pipeline (ResolveCustomer, BatchMeterUsage, EventBridge via SAM) and querying/debugging metering records, statuses, failures, discrepancies. Covers Concurrent Agreements (CustomerAWSAccountId + LicenseArn), multi-product, CloudTrail Event History (90 days; never CloudTrail Lake), Seller Reports (Billed Revenue/Usage columns + CSV/SDDS parsing), existing-stack detection, metering region for BatchMeterUsage/MeterUsage (DisabledApiException), metered AMI/Container + IAM roles, where registration data lives (customer-profile table), legacy ProductCode + CustomerIdentifier, DuplicateRecord/CustomerNotSubscribed, bad LicenseArn. Also SCOPES adjacent asks — flat-rate/SaaS-Subscription \"do I still meter\", CCP included-units + overage, contract term/annual renewal, forecasting future bills/usage — with the right answer or a one-line redirect. Use for any BatchMeterUsage/ResolveCustomer or SaaS usage-billing question.\n\nServices: aws-marketplace, marketplace-metering\nTasks: deploy, integrate, query, debug, troubleshoot, analyze\nPersona: developer\nWorkload: saas\n*","skill_name":"aws-marketplace-metering"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify AWS Carbon Footprint API GroupBy dimensions and data availability details"}
